<h2>The background</h2>
<p>The plaintiff society runs &ldquo;The Aryan School&rdquo; in Dehradun with registered marks in Class 41; it sued the defendant, which runs &ldquo;Aaryan International School&rdquo;, and obtained an ex parte decree in October 2024 — a permanent injunction with Rs 5,00,000 in damages and costs. The defendant had been served by speed post and was set ex parte in September 2024.</p>
<p>The defendant claimed it learnt of the decree only in February 2025, when a Local Commissioner arrived, and moved to set the decree aside 196 days out of time. Its explanation: it had engaged an advocate back in March 2024, who &ldquo;assured&rdquo; it the matter was being handled.</p>

<h2>Proving the engagement is the threshold</h2>
<p>The court&rsquo;s analysis is unusually clean because the defendant&rsquo;s file was unusually empty. Service was admitted. The entire sufficient-cause case was the advocate story — and there was nothing behind it:</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment:</strong> &ldquo;there is no document to establish that the counsel was, in fact, engaged, instructed or duly authorised&rdquo; &mdash; no vakalatnama, no engagement letter, no fee receipt, no correspondence.</p>
</div>
<p>On the authorities — <em>Rajneesh Kumar</em>, <em>Moddus Media</em>, <em>A. Murugesan</em> — a litigant must remain vigilant about its own case; counsel&rsquo;s negligence can excuse a default only where the engagement and the litigant&rsquo;s diligence are shown. Orders in the suit had been available online for over seven months. As the court put it, the &ldquo;Defendant cannot claim that it was unaware of case status and/or non-appearance of the counsel&rdquo;. The 196-day delay fell with the same reasoning, and an arrest in the family of the defendant&rsquo;s chairperson, pressed as an explanation, post-dated the decree.</p>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For defendants in commercial suits.</strong> The paper of an engagement — vakalatnama, receipt, emails — is not clerical tidiness; it is the evidence that decides an Order IX Rule 13 application years later. If the lawyer truly defaulted, the file proves it; if the file is empty, the plea reads as invention.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>On vigilance.</strong> Case status in Delhi is a public, online fact. Courts now treat months of online accessibility as itself answering a claim of ignorance. A party served with a commercial suit must track it or bear the consequences.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For decree-holders.</strong> Meet a counsel-negligence plea with the engagement question first — before arguing diligence or merits. Where the engagement is unproved, the application fails at the threshold and the merits never open.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>Order IX Rule 13 jurisprudence balances two goods: the finality of decrees and the policy of deciding cases on merits. The balance has been shifting, in commercial litigation especially, towards finality — driven by the Commercial Courts Act&rsquo;s timelines and by the courts&rsquo; impatience with reconstructed explanations. This judgment is a sharp example: the court did not disbelieve that lawyers sometimes fail clients; it held that the claim must be proved like any other fact, and that a defendant&rsquo;s own two-year silence, against an online docket, speaks louder than an unsupported assurance it attributes to an unnamed file. For schools and businesses trading under similar names, it is also a reminder that trademark suits ignored do not go away — they become decrees.</p>
